This add-on assumes the FEAST package to be available and installed.
FEAST can be downloaded from https://www.feast-solver.org.
Read the documentation of FEAST carefully for the installation and use.
Note that:
 * FEAST requires MKL-PARDISO and therefore does not run natively on
   Apple Silicon Macs. However, it does run in a Docker container build
   for amd64 on Apple Silicon Macs using all processors available.
 * Should work with gfortran, but I got a compile error. Hence only tested
   with ifx, which worked.
 * Currently, only the real generalized eigenvalue routine of FEAST for
   sparse matrices with factorization is used.

INSTALLATION & USAGE::
====================

- Use a recent version of MKL, which is part of Intel OpenAPI.

- Adapt Mdefs.mk to link to the FEAST library, if not done already.

- Build tfem using:

    make all feast

  for a usual build + feast.

- For a real generalized eigenvalue problem (A-lambda B) v=0:

  program myprog

    ...

    use feast_m

    ...

    type(solver_options_feast_t) :: so
    type(arg_feast_t) :: arg

    ...

    (set optional parameters in structure so)
    (set input arguments in structure arg)

    call solve_system_real_gen_feast ( sysmatrixA, sysmatrixB, arg, &
      solver_options=so )

    ...

    (postprocessing output arguments in structure arg)

    ...

    call delete ( arg )

  end program myprog

- Use the environment variable OMP_NUM_THREADS to specify the number of
  processors available for the solver, similarly to pardiso.


Remarks:
=======

- See code of feast_m and the examples for the parameters that can be set
  in the structures so and arg. Also in the examples it is shown how to obtain
  the eigenvalues.

- FEAST uses MKL-PARDISO for the handling of sparse-CSR matrices and a direct
  sparse solver. This means that the sequential library of MKL cannot be used.
  Like when using pardiso, the threaded library needs to be linked.
